 "caption": "Pieter Bruegel painted this Flemish village in 1559, and every person in it is acting out a saying. By most counts there are more than a hundred.\n\nA man in armour bangs his head against a brick wall. A woman ties the devil to a pillow. One man shears a sheep, the other a pig. Roses are thrown to the swine, the roof is tiled with tarts, the globe hangs upside down, and a monk ties a fake beard on the Lord.\n\nAnd right in the middle, a woman in red pulls a blue cloak over her husband so he can't see a thing. In Bruegel's day a blue cloak meant deceit: she is cheating on him. That is the painting's old name: The Blue Cloak, or The Folly of the World.\n\nP.S. His son, Pieter Brueghel the Younger, painted at least 16 copies of it.\n\n🖤 Which proverb did you spot first?",
 "caption_short": "This painting has a secret name, hidden in the crowd. Bruegel's Netherlandish Proverbs, 1559. 🖤",
